{
 "units": [
  {
   "id": "u1",
   "title": "The Khmer script",
   "subtitle": "Consonants, their two series, and vowel signs",
   "km": "ក ខ គ",
   "lessons": [
    {
     "id": "script-1",
     "title": "Consonants 1",
     "icon": "ក",
     "consonants": [
      "c01",
      "c02",
      "c03",
      "c04",
      "c05",
      "c06",
      "c07",
      "c08"
     ],
     "tip": "Every consonant has an inherent vowel. a-series letters (like ក kâ) read with â [ɑː]; o-series letters (like គ kô) read with ô [ɔː]."
    },
    {
     "id": "script-2",
     "title": "Consonants 2",
     "icon": "ញ",
     "consonants": [
      "c09",
      "c10",
      "c11",
      "c12",
      "c13",
      "c14",
      "c15",
      "c16"
     ],
     "tip": "ឋ, ឌ and ឍ are rare and appear mostly in words borrowed from Pali and Sanskrit."
    },
    {
     "id": "script-3",
     "title": "Consonants 3",
     "icon": "ប",
     "consonants": [
      "c17",
      "c18",
      "c19",
      "c20",
      "c21",
      "c22",
      "c23",
      "c24"
     ],
     "tip": "ប bâ is [ɓ] before a vowel, but [p] at the end of a syllable."
    },
    {
     "id": "script-4",
     "title": "Consonants 4",
     "icon": "ស",
     "consonants": [
      "c25",
      "c26",
      "c27",
      "c28",
      "c29",
      "c30",
      "c31",
      "c32",
      "c33"
     ],
     "tip": "អ ’â stands for a glottal stop and is the letter vowel signs are written on when a word starts with a vowel sound."
    },
    {
     "id": "vowels-1",
     "title": "Vowel signs 1",
     "icon": "អា",
     "vowels": [
      "v01",
      "v02",
      "v03",
      "v04",
      "v05",
      "v06",
      "v07",
      "v08"
     ],
     "tip": "A vowel sign sounds different on a-series and o-series consonants. ា after ក is [aː]; after គ it is [iːə]."
    },
    {
     "id": "vowels-2",
     "title": "Vowel signs 2",
     "icon": "អៅ",
     "vowels": [
      "v09",
      "v10",
      "v11",
      "v12",
      "v13",
      "v14",
      "v15",
      "v16"
     ],
     "tip": "Vowel signs can sit before, above, below or around the consonant, but are always read after it."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "u2",
   "title": "Greetings & politeness",
   "subtitle": "Hello, thank you, yes and no",
   "km": "សួស្តី",
   "lessons": [
    {
     "id": "greet-1",
     "title": "Hello & goodbye",
     "icon": "👋",
     "items": [
      "hello",
      "hello-formal",
      "good-morning",
      "goodbye",
      "goodbye-formal",
      "see-you-later"
     ],
     "tip": "ជម្រាបសួរ is the respectful hello, usually said with a sampeah: palms together in front of the chest."
    },
    {
     "id": "greet-2",
     "title": "Thank you & sorry",
     "icon": "🙏",
     "items": [
      "thank-you",
      "sorry",
      "its-ok",
      "no-problem",
      "please",
      "invite",
      "sampeah"
     ],
     "sentences": [
      "thanks-a-lot"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "greet-3",
     "title": "Yes & no",
     "icon": "✅",
     "items": [
      "yes-m",
      "yes-f",
      "no",
      "not",
      "not-colloquial"
     ],
     "tip": "Men say បាទ baat for yes; women say ចាស caah."
    },
    {
     "id": "greet-4",
     "title": "You & me",
     "icon": "🧑",
     "items": [
      "i",
      "you",
      "bong",
      "oun",
      "lok",
      "lok-srey",
      "uncle",
      "aunt"
     ],
     "tip": "Khmer uses family words for \"you\". Call someone a bit older បង bɑɑng, someone younger ប្អូន pʼoun, a man you respect លោក look."
    },
    {
     "id": "greet-5",
     "title": "How are you?",
     "icon": "😊",
     "items": [
      "well",
      "happy",
      "be",
      "name",
      "what",
      "question-marker"
     ],
     "sentences": [
      "how-are-you",
      "im-fine",
      "whats-your-name"
     ],
     "tip": "សុខសប្បាយ sok sapbaay means \"well and happy\". Ask it as a question by adding ជាទេ."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "u3",
   "title": "Numbers",
   "subtitle": "Khmer digits and counting to a thousand",
   "km": "១ ២ ៣",
   "lessons": [
    {
     "id": "num-1",
     "title": "One to five",
     "icon": "១",
     "digits": [
      "d0",
      "d1",
      "d2",
      "d3",
      "d4",
      "d5"
     ],
     "items": [
      "zero",
      "one",
      "two",
      "three",
      "four",
      "five"
     ],
     "tip": "Khmer has its own digits, ០ to ៩, written just like Western numbers: ១២ is 12."
    },
    {
     "id": "num-2",
     "title": "Six to ten",
     "icon": "៦",
     "digits": [
      "d6",
      "d7",
      "d8",
      "d9"
     ],
     "items": [
      "six",
      "seven",
      "eight",
      "nine",
      "ten"
     ],
     "tip": "Six to nine are \"five-one\" to \"five-four\": ប្រាំ pram + មួយ muəy = 6."
    },
    {
     "id": "num-3",
     "title": "Tens",
     "icon": "៣០",
     "items": [
      "twenty",
      "thirty",
      "forty",
      "fifty",
      "sixty",
      "seventy",
      "eighty",
      "ninety"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "num-4",
     "title": "Bigger numbers",
     "icon": "១០០",
     "items": [
      "hundred",
      "thousand",
      "number",
      "eleven",
      "fifteen",
      "twenty-one",
      "one-hundred",
      "one-thousand"
     ],
     "tip": "Numbers in between are built from the parts: ម្ភៃ mphɨy (20) + មួយ muəy (1) = 21."
    }
   ]
  },
